 "description": "A 300 mm cube frame of 20 mm rails with a gantry beam across the top and slot nuts in it. One techsquid-extrusions run stands four 300 mm T-slot 2020 uprights on the points of the sketch \"Upright points\". Two more runs lay eight 260 mm T-slot 2020 rails between them, bottom and top: four along X from a sketch on the YZ plane and four along Y from a sketch on the XZ plane. The second of those runs finds the rail the first one built and places it again. A last run puts a 260 mm V-slot 2040 beam on edge between the top side rails. A rail's section is centred on its point, with its width along the sketch X and its height along the sketch Y, and the rail runs out of the sketch along its normal, so the plane and offset of each sketch say which way its rails run and where they start. Two runs of the plugin's other entry, make-t-nut, then put slot nuts in the frame: four M5 post-assembly nuts in the top slots of the two top cross rails, from points on a sketch on top of the frame, and two M5 pre-assembly nuts in the lower front slot of the gantry beam. A nut hangs under the lips of the slot with its tapped hole on the point, and `along` says which sketch axis the rail runs along. The rails and nuts stay where the points were when the plugin ran, and no screw, bracket or joint is modeled. Each step is one command; `caption` is the text a recording shows for it. A plugin step is a `plugin.run` whose `path` is relative to the repository and whose `approval` is the placeholder `@approval`: tests/examples_test.py fills both in, because the approval changes with every build of the plugin. `expect` is what that test measures: a rail's `volume_mm3` is the area of its section times its length, and a nut's is what the build measured.",
